<h2>Earthquake risk along the Wasatch Front</h2>
<p>On March 18, 2020, a magnitude 5.7 earthquake centered near Magna shook the Salt Lake Valley. Chimneys toppled, brick parapets failed and many older homes cracked. Geologists consider a larger Wasatch Fault earthquake a long-term possibility, and much of Utah’s older brick housing is vulnerable.</p>
<h2>Coverage comes first</h2>
<p>Standard homeowners policies typically exclude earth movement. Earthquake coverage is usually added by endorsement or purchased as a separate policy, often with a deductible of 10% or more of the dwelling limit. Appraisal only helps when coverage applies and the dispute is about the amount.</p>
<h2>What earthquake valuations need</h2>
<ul>
<li>Careful documentation separating new earthquake cracking from older settling.</li>
<li>Masonry and chimney repair or rebuild, including bracing and code requirements.</li>
<li>Foundation evaluation, sometimes with an engineer’s report.</li>
<li>Interior drywall and plaster cracking throughout the home, not only in the worst rooms.</li>
<li>Pricing that accounts for limited contractor availability after a regional event.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Deductible math</b>On a $500,000 dwelling limit, a 15% earthquake deductible is $75,000. Use our <a href="/utah-claim-calculators/percentage-deductible-calculator/">percentage deductible calculator</a> to see whether a disputed amount actually changes what you’d receive.</div>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Does homeowners insurance cover earthquakes in Utah?<span class="plus" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="ans"><p>Usually not without an endorsement or separate policy. Check your declarations page for earthquake coverage and its deductible.</p></div></details><details><summary>How do I prove cracks are from the earthquake?<span class="plus" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="ans"><p>Before-and-after photos, inspection records and an engineer’s evaluation are the strongest evidence. Fresh, clean crack edges and patterns consistent with shaking also help.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is it worth appraising if I’m near my deductible?<span class="plus" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="ans"><p>Sometimes. If the disputed amount pushes the loss well above the deductible, it can make a real difference. If not, appraisal costs may outweigh the benefit.</p></div></details></div><h2>Related services</h2><div class="rel-grid"><a class="rel" href="/utah-appraisal-services/insurance-estimate-audit/"><small>Service</small><span>Insurance Estimate Review</span><svg class="ico sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a class="rel" href="/utah-appraisal-services/appraisal-umpire-services/"><small>Service</small><span>Umpire Services</span><svg class="ico sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a class="rel" href="/utah-appraisal-services/policy-appraisal-representation/"><small>Service</small><span>Appraisal Clause Representation</span><svg class="ico sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke